WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.152
In the last section, the tally ran out of memory: a hash map keeps one line per different key, and there were too many keys.

2
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:12.955
Sorting looks like it has the same problem.

3
00:00:12.955 --> 00:00:16.917
To put keys in order, surely you need them all in memory at once?

4
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.702
A sort done in memory does, and it would fail exactly like the hash map.

5
00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:22.852
Hold that thought.

6
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:26.995
Back at the ballot table, picture a pile too big for the table.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.388
Take an armful, sort it by name, and set the sorted stack aside.

8
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:35.511
Take the next armful, and the next, until the pile is gone.

9
00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:42.299
Then merge the stacks: look at the top ballot of each, take the one that comes first in the alphabet, and repeat.

10
00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.731
Equal names come out side by side, and you count them as they pass.

11
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:47.687
Map it out.

12
00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:49.330
The table is the memory.

13
00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.316
An armful is what fits in it.

14
00:00:51.316 --> 00:00:53.782
Each sorted stack is a file on disk.

15
00:00:53.782 --> 00:00:57.548
And the merge only ever looks at the top of each stack.

16
00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:59.068
Let's shrink it.

17
00:00:59.068 --> 00:01:07.249
A web server's log records a status code for every request, a number like 200 for OK or 404 for not found.

18
00:01:07.249 --> 00:01:11.571
Here are twelve of them, and a memory that holds only 4.

19
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:16.583
Read the first 4, sort them in memory, and write them to a temporary file.

20
00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:19.611
A sorted file like this is called a run.

21
00:01:19.833 --> 00:01:22.315
Two more batches give runs 2 and 3.

22
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:27.665
The input has been read once, and every key sits in a sorted run.

23
00:01:27.665 --> 00:01:29.948
The memory never held more than 4.

24
00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:33.105
It's a very short table, and it never complained.

25
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:34.540
Now the merge.

26
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.214
Our merge reads only 2 runs at once.

27
00:01:37.214 --> 00:01:38.329
We have 3 runs.

28
00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:42.563
How many rounds of merging until one sorted file is left?

29
00:01:42.563 --> 00:01:43.752
Pause and guess.

30
00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:47.249
Two.

31
00:01:47.249 --> 00:01:49.087
Pass 1 opens runs 1 and 2.

32
00:01:49.087 --> 00:01:57.355
It keeps the first key of each in a small priority queue, a structure that always hands back its smallest item first.

33
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:02.943
Write the smaller one, refill from the run it came from, and repeat.

34
00:02:02.943 --> 00:02:04.693
200, 200, 201, and on.

35
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:09.169
Memory holds one key per open run, however long the runs are.

36
00:02:09.433 --> 00:02:12.686
Runs 1 and 2 become run 4, with 8 keys.

37
00:02:12.686 --> 00:02:15.522
Run 3 had no partner, so it waits.

38
00:02:15.522 --> 00:02:16.856
3 runs became 2.

39
00:02:17.033 --> 00:02:21.904
Pass 2 merges run 4 with run 3, and writes the final sorted file.

40
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:29.331
Each pass read and wrote every key once, from the start of each file to its end, the way disks read fastest.

41
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:46.417
Equal keys are now neighbours, so uniq, a small program that collapses repeated lines, counts them with a single counter: 200 comes three times, 201 five times, 402 twice, 404 and 500 once each.

42
00:02:46.567 --> 00:02:49.624
At no step did the sort hold more than 4 keys.

43
00:02:49.833 --> 00:02:56.155
This is called an external merge sort: external because the runs live outside memory, on disk.

44
00:02:56.533 --> 00:02:58.646
Now the real numbers.

45
00:02:58.646 --> 00:03:03.373
100 GB of keys and a 1 GB buffer give 100 runs.

46
00:03:03.567 --> 00:03:07.904
The number of runs one merge reads at once is the fan-in.

47
00:03:07.904 --> 00:03:14.447
With a fan-in of 16, the first pass leaves 7 runs and the second leaves 1: two passes.

48
00:03:14.447 --> 00:03:15.512
With 128, one.

49
00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:18.402
Every pass reads and writes all 100 GB.

50
00:03:18.402 --> 00:03:26.282
So a sort costs more processor work and extra trips over the disk, and in exchange it never runs out of memory.

51
00:03:26.282 --> 00:03:29.051
It only gets slower as the input grows.

52
00:03:29.233 --> 00:03:42.168
GNU sort, the sort program on Linux, does all this on its own: with a 4 MB buffer, 46 MB of keys made 73 runs, and it never held more than about 7 MB.

53
00:03:42.533 --> 00:03:50.561
So the moral of the story: when the keys don't fit, sort an armful at a time, and merge by looking only at the top of each run.

54
00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:52.555
The tally falls off a cliff.

55
00:03:52.555 --> 00:03:54.855
The sort just walks a little slower.

56
00:03:55.233 --> 00:03:56.430
One quick check.

57
00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.692
With a fan-in of 2, how many merge passes do 5 runs need?

58
00:04:00.692 --> 00:04:01.888
Pause and think.

59
00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:05.715
Three.

60
00:04:05.715 --> 00:04:08.816
5 runs become 3, then 2, then 1.
